{"agent":"lane-kl-kill-cause","data":{"budget":"1 h, no GPU","claim":"Blocking the three signals in the main thread and reading them with signal.sigtimedwait on a watcher thread yields si_pid/si_uid; /proc/<pid>/comm|status names the sender and its ancestry; a SIGUSR2 pthread_kill wakes the main thread so the existing RunTerminated path records the outcome; unblocking around Popen keeps the command's SigBlk clear.","dedupe":{"answer":"new","candidates":[],"score":null},"falsifier":"A helper process kills a running gate run with SIGTERM and the recorded evidence does not carry sender_pid == helper pid; or the run's child shows SIGTERM in SigBlk.","item":"KL-kill-cause","mechanism":"A process-directed signal blocked in every thread stays pending and is consumed by sigtimedwait, which returns the full siginfo (Python handlers never see it). The signal mask is inherited across exec, so the spawn path must unblock for the child.","probe":"pytest tests/test_gate_kill_facts.py (before the change, then after)","probe_output":"before: KeyError: 'kill' in test_a_sigterm_from_a_helper_names_the_helper_as_sender and the SIGHUP test (5 failed); after: 7 passed, including sender_pid == helper.pid for SIGTERM/SIGHUP/SIGINT and no SigBlk bit for TERM/HUP/INT in the gate command's children.","question":"Can a gate run's owner read the sender (si_pid) of a SIGTERM/SIGHUP/SIGINT and still record the outcome, without leaving the gate command unkillable?","sources":[],"verdict":"CONFIRMED"},"id":"e07b770255269e272a52c32ad","kind":"research.recorded","lamport":195731,"schema":1,"subject":"R-kl-sigtimedwait-sender","ts":"2026-11-13T19:01:34.231003Z"}
